.SH BUGS
.PP
This is Bluetooth Low Energy only.  There is no Bluetooth Classic:
no inquiry, no SDP, no RFCOMM, no HID, no PAN, and no audio.  L2CAP
here is the fixed channels an LE link uses \- ATT, LE signalling and
security management \- and the length and CID framing under them;
there are no connection-oriented channels.
.PP
Pairing is LE legacy pairing with a zero temporary key, which is Just
Works: it establishes an encrypted link and a bond, and it gives no
protection at all against a man in the middle.  There is no passkey
entry and no numeric comparison.  LE Secure Connections is not
implemented \- it needs P-256 elliptic curve Diffie-Hellman, which is
not here \- so a device that insists on it will not pair, and says so.
.PP
The USB transport
.RB ( -u )
is written and compiles but has never moved a packet.  There is no
USB Bluetooth controller on the bench and no emulated one, so nothing
about it is demonstrated; only the H4 transport is.
.PP
On
.B pc64
the H4 transport over the second serial port makes the kernel fault:
sustained traffic on that uart ends in a general protection violation
at the
.B IRETQ
in
.B _intrr
.RB ( os/inferno/pc64/l.s ),
with a corrupt interrupt frame.  That is a fault in the kernel's own
interrupt return, not in this stack: the same Dis code over a
.IR dial (2)
transport completes on the same
.B pc64
kernel and on
.BR riscv64 .
See
.B doc/inferno-ports.ms.
.PP
Only the initiating (central) side is implemented.  This stack does
not advertise and cannot be connected to.
.PP
A resolvable private address is resolved against every stored bond's
identity resolving key, so a device that rotates its address \- which
most modern peripherals do \- stays one device under one directory,
named by its identity, and the bond made with it remains reachable.
Resolution is done here rather than by handing the keys to the
controller's resolving list.
.PP
The name a directory is given when the device is first heard from is
kept.  So a device first seen from a private address, and paired only
afterwards, keeps the directory named by that first private address
even though its later addresses resolve to it: the name is settled
before there is a key to resolve it with, and a directory a client may
be holding open is not renamed underneath it.  Its
.B identity
file says what it really is.
.PP
A bond read from the
.B -k
key file at startup resolves addresses just as one made in the session
does, so a device bonded with earlier is recognised again without
pairing.
.PP
Pairing is still LE legacy Just Works, so the identity key is
distributed without protection against a man in the middle, and
resolution is only as trustworthy as the pairing that produced the
key.
.PP
.B scripts/btbench.sh
runs
.I btbench
under
.I emu
against the controller model in
.B scripts/bthci.py ,
in two phases -- pairing, then meeting the bond again from a key file
\- and exits non-zero if either fails.
.PP
Each phase is also captured.
.B bthci.py --snoop
writes every HCI packet, both directions, as
.BR btsnoop ,
and the bench runs
.I tshark
over the capture and fails the phase on an expert warning or error it
did not declare.  A phase that injects a fault a controller may not
commit \- 
.B params
sends a connection parameter request that cannot be honoured \- names
the one complaint it is allowed to draw, by its text, so any other
still fails it.  A capture holding no packets fails too, since that is
what a capture that was never written looks like.
Every other check in the bench is this stack talking to a model
written from the same specifications by the same reader, so a
misreading agrees with itself and passes; Wireshark's dissectors were
written by other people, and are the one check here that does not
share that reading.  They found three faults that the bench had been
passing: a Command Complete answering
.BR HCI_Inquiry ,
which is defined to answer with a Command Status; a frame whose
ethertype said ARP carrying a byte pattern; and a media stream that
had agreed to SBC carrying bytes that were not SBC.  Notes are not
failures \-
.B "Reached ATT_MTU"
is what a dissector says about a value longer than one packet, which
the bench sends on purpose.  Without
.I tshark
the captures are still written and nothing reads them, and the bench
says so as it starts.
.PP
.B scripts/btvirt/run.sh
runs one phase against BlueZ instead of against the model.  It builds
a container holding
.IR btvirt ,
BlueZ's controller emulator, with a peer built out of BlueZ's own host
stack and GATT server on a second controller, and points
.I btbench
at it over the same
.B tcp!
transport the model uses \(em so the controller, the link layer, the
host, the SMP and the GATT server are all code this tree did not
write.  The
.B btvirt
phase is the one written for that peer, and it covers both halves:
scanning, connecting, discovery, a read, notifications, a long write,
pairing and a credit-based channel on LE, then an inquiry, a page, an
L2CAP channel, a serial port over RFCOMM and secure simple pairing on
BR/EDR.  Each of the last two answers in
upper case rather than echoing, so the reply has to have been read by
the far end.
.B full
fails against it, because it ends by watching a device rotate a
resolvable private address and this peer does not rotate one.
.PP
The image carries one patch,
.BR scripts/btvirt/bthost-rfcomm-length.patch .
BlueZ's test host stack writes the RFCOMM header's length into the
length indicator, which TS 07.10 defines as the information field's
length alone; its own receiver reads the field the defined way and so
refuses frames its own sender builds.  It
needs Docker, which is why it is not part of
.BR btbench.sh .
.PP
.B doc/bluetooth-plan.ms
lists what is missing in the order the pieces block each other, and
plans the 9front implementation.
